<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Madelon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first performed 19 March <a href="/years/1914">1914</a>; first recorded <a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Quand Madelon...</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Quand Madelon...&quot;, usually called &quot;La Madelon&quot;, has words by Louis Bousquet (1870–1941) and music by Camille Robert (1872–1957). In 1913 the singer Bach (Charles-Joseph Pasquier) asked them for a new patriotic song; the music began as a march for a brass band, to which Bousquet wrote cheerful words. Bach first sang it on 19 March 1914 at the café-concert l'Eldorado in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where it had little success.</p></div>
